  "Question": "The equation \\[ x-\\frac7{x-3}=3-\\frac7{x-3} \\] has:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "infinitely many integral roots",
    "B": "no root",
    "C": "one integral root",
    "D": "two equal integral roots",
    "E": "two equal non-integral roots"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "First note that \\(x\\ne3\\), because the denominator \\(x-3\\) cannot be zero. For any allowed value of \\(x\\), add \\(\\frac7{x-3}\\) to both sides. The identical fractional terms cancel, leaving\n\\[\nx=3.\n\\]\nBut this value makes the original equation undefined. Therefore the equation has \\(\\boxed{\\text{no root}}\\), choice \\(\\mathrm{B}\\).",
